class:
public class Person { }  // Defines a class named "Person"
public class Employee extends Person { }  // Defines a subclass


extends:
public class Employee extends Person { }
// Employee inherits all non-private members from Person


2. Access Modifiers
public
Accessible from anywhere - any class can access.
public class Person { }           // Class accessible everywhere
public String name;               // Field accessible everywhere
public void introduce() { }       // Method accessible everywhere

private

Accessible only within the same class.
private String name;              // Only Person class can access
private int age;                  // Only Person class can access


3. Variable-Related Keywords
static

Belongs to the class, not instances. Shared across all objects.
java

private static int totalPersons = 0;  // One variable shared by all Person objects
// Access: Person.totalPersons (not person1.totalPersons)

final

Value cannot be changed after assignment (constant).
java

final double PI = 3.14159;         // Cannot be reassigned
final String COMPANY_NAME = "ABC"; // Constant

this

Refers to the current instance of the class.
java

this.name = name;    // Distinguishes instance variable from parameter
this.age = age;      // Refers to "this" object's age

super

Refers to the parent class.
java

super(name, age, email);  // Calls parent class constructor
super.toString();         // Calls parent's toString() method

4. Method-Related Keywords
void

Indicates a method returns nothing.
java

public void introduce() { }      // No return value
public void birthday() { }       // No return value

return

Exits a method and optionally returns a value.
java

public String getName() { 
    return name;                 // Returns the name value
}
public int getAge() { 
    return age;                  // Returns the age value
}

@Override

Annotation indicating a method overrides a parent class method.
java

@Override
public String toString() { }     // Overrides Object's toString()

5. Object Creation & Type Keywords
new

Creates a new instance (object) of a class.
java

Person person1 = new Person("Alice", 25, "alice@email.com");
//              ^^^ Creates a new Person object in memory

instanceof

Checks if an object is an instance of a particular class.
java

if (person instanceof Employee) {    // Is 'person' an Employee?
    Employee e = (Employee) person;  // Safe to cast
}

6. Control Flow Keywords
if / else

Conditional execution.
java

if (person instanceof Employee) {
    // Execute this block if condition is true
} else {
    // Execute this block if condition is false
}

for

Loop through a block of code a specified number of times.
java

for (Person p : people) {    // Enhanced for-each loop
    System.out.println(p);
}

7. Data Type Keywords
Primitive Types
java

int age = 25;          // 32-bit integer
double salary = 75000; // 64-bit floating point
boolean isValid = true; // true or false
char grade = 'A';      // 16-bit Unicode character
byte b = 127;          // 8-bit integer
short s = 1000;        // 16-bit integer
long l = 100000L;      // 64-bit integer (with L suffix)
float f = 3.14f;       // 32-bit floating point (with f suffix)

Reference Types
java

String name;           // Class type (String is a class)
Person person;         // Custom class type
Employee emp;          // Custom class type
Person[] people;       // Array of Person objects

8. Package & Import Keywords
package (not shown but important)

Defines the package/namespace for the class.
java

package com.example.myapp;

import (not shown)

Imports classes from other packages.
java

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

9. Exception Handling Keywords (partially shown)
throws (not shown)

Declares that a method might throw an exception.
java

public void readFile() throws IOException { }

try / catch / finally (not shown)

Handle exceptions.
java

try {
    // Risky code
} catch (Exception e) {
    // Handle exception
} finally {
    // Always executed
}

10. Keywords in the Inheritance Example
super (in constructor)
java

super(name, age, email);  // Must be first line in constructor
// Calls Person's constructor with these parameters

@Override (annotation)
java

@Override
public String toString() { }  // Tells compiler this overrides parent method

Complete Keyword Reference Table
Keyword	Purpose	Example
class	Defines a class	class Person { }
public	Accessible everywhere	public String name;
private	Accessible only in class	private int age;
static	Belongs to class, not instance	static int count;
final	Cannot be changed	final double PI = 3.14;
void	Returns nothing	void print() { }
return	Returns from method	return value;
new	Creates object	new Person();
this	Current instance	this.name = name;
super	Parent class	super(name);
extends	Inheritance	class Employee extends Person
instanceof	Type check	obj instanceof Person
if	Conditional	if (x > 5) { }
else	Alternative	else { }
for	Loop	for (int i=0; i<10; i++)
int	32-bit integer	int num = 5;
double	64-bit float	double d = 3.14;
boolean	true/false	boolean flag = true;
String	Text/string	String text = "Hello";
@Override	Override annotation	@Override void run()
